{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>You shall not steal, etc.</b> It placed the commandment of stealing adjacent to the commandment of the corner of the field [pe'ah]. Perhaps it intends this along the lines of what they said in Torat Kohanim (here), and this is their language: Ben Bag Bag says: 'You shall not steal — [do not steal] what is yours from the thief, so that you do not appear as a thief.' End quote. And here it intends, in what it placed adjacent ״לֹא תִּגְנֹבוּ״ to pe'ah, that one is warned about it not to steal it, thinking that it is his, and taking it." "And the explanation of these three prohibitions is: <b>לֹא תִּגְנֹבוּ</b> — this is one who takes his fellow's money without his knowledge. <b>לֹא תְכַחֲשׁוּ</b> — this is one whose fellow's money has come into his possession by permitted means, and he denies it when the other claims it from him. <b>לֹא תְשַׁקְּרוּ</b> — this is one whose fellow's money has come into his possession with witnesses, and he denies it; even though his denial is of no avail to him in monetary terms, he nevertheless transgresses on account of ״לֹא תְשַׁקְּרוּ״." "<b>Further,</b> it alludes that if a person transgressed the first prohibition and stole, the Holy One, blessed be He, will arrange for him acquaintances [who know], along the lines of their saying, of blessed memory (Ḥagigah 16a; Numbers Rabbah ch. 9), that one who transgresses in secret — the Holy One, blessed be He, proclaims it and publicizes his wickedness. If so, then if he stole — HaShem will arrange someone who will know of his theft, and HaShem commands that person not to deny it; and that is His saying <b>לֹא תְכַחֲשׁוּ</b>. And Scripture speaks in a manner where the witness is able to deny it, such as where there is only one witness — if he admits, well and good; and if the witness denies, the Holy One, blessed be He, will arrange a second witness for him who will testify against him so that he cannot deny it; and if he does deny, it is falsehood in His right hand and his lies will not avail him — and that is His saying <b>לֹא תְשַׁקְּרוּ</b>, for besides the fact that his deeds did not avail him, behold he also transgresses this prohibition." "And the reason for His using plural language, which He did not use in this sequence until now — and also in the commandments that follow it He used singular language, as it is written ״לֹא תַעֲשֹׁק״ etc. — it appears that He intended to warn even in a case where one person recognizes that his fellow stole, or denied, etc., that he should not say, 'I will do the same to save my own.' Therefore He said it in plural language: <b>לֹא תִּגְנֹבוּ</b> — meaning, even though he stole, you shall not add to act wickedly like him, and the two of you will become thieves. And likewise if your fellow denies, you shall not also act as he does and both of you deny. And likewise if your fellow lied to you, etc. And He said <b>וְלֹא תִשָּׁבְעוּ</b> as well, so that one should not say, if he knows that his fellow swore falsely to him, 'I hereby swear to save my money from him,' and they may swear under duress — for behold you are profaning the Name, etc. And therefore He concluded with singular language, to say that He is speaking to the one who saves his own money through an oath, and needless to say the one who swore first, who is alluded to within ״לֹא תִשָּׁבְעוּ״. Further, in His saying <b>לֹא תִשָּׁבְעוּ</b> He intends to warn also the one who administers the oath: if he knows that his fellow is swearing falsely, he should not make him swear; and it is not a profanation except on the part of the one who swears — therefore He said <b>וְחִלַּלְתָּ</b> in singular language."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:59:04.397816245Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}